  "response": "Adjudicating between an impact trigger and a volcanic one is, in the first instance, a problem of chronology and of geochemical fingerprinting. Investigators date candidate triggers by uranium-lead and argon-argon methods, correlate those ages with the stratigraphic level at which taxa vanish, distinguish markers of global reach from those of merely regional extent, and model the atmospheric consequences of each mechanism against proxy records of temperature, ocean chemistry and biological productivity. A proposed trigger survives scrutiny only if its timing coincides with the extinction horizon within analytical uncertainty and its environmental signature matches the observed pattern of loss.\n\nThe end-Cretaceous extinction eliminated roughly 75 percent of species on Earth. The disappearance of non-avian dinosaurs from the fossil record at the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary is the most familiar evidence of a loss on that scale, since a clade dominant for well over a hundred million years terminates abruptly at that horizon.\n\nOne group of researchers attributes the end-Cretaceous extinction primarily to Deccan Traps volcanism. The Deccan Traps flood basalt province is located in western India. Deccan Traps eruptions released large volumes of sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide into the atmosphere, and that combination of short-term cooling and long-term warming agents indicates that the volcanic driver was of sufficient magnitude to account for the collapse. The Deccan Traps erupted during the Jurassic period, tens of millions of years before the boundary layer formed, a chronology that cannot be squared with assigning the extinction to those lavas.\n\nWalter Alvarez and colleagues reported an anomalous iridium concentration in boundary clay at Gubbio, Italy, in 1980, a measurement that made an extraterrestrial contribution to the boundary clay a testable proposition. The boundary iridium anomaly was first detected in New Zealand sediments in 1965; a priority of that kind cannot be reconciled with the standing of the Gubbio measurement as the original recognition of the anomaly.\n\nEither the Cretaceous-Paleogene iridium anomaly has been documented at only a single locality on Earth, or it has been documented at more than one locality: one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both hold. Its occurrence at more than one locality, rather than as an isolated local enrichment, shows that the iridium was delivered by a single planet-wide event, and thereby supplies the evidential basis for the impact attribution. Pressures attainable only by hypervelocity impact are registered in shocked quartz grains at the Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary, evidence that vindicates another group of researchers in attributing the end-Cretaceous extinction primarily to the Chicxulub impact. That attribution was stated in so many words by a 2010 review panel of 41 scientists writing in Science, which concluded that the Chicxulub impact caused the end-Cretaceous mass extinction. After that panel's verdict appeared, IODP-ICDP Expedition 364 drilled into the peak ring of the Chicxulub crater in 2016.",
